course: dictionary
lesson: 14582
title: Plagiarised: copied and passed off as your own
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Plagiarised means copied from someone else's work and passed off as your own, without saying where it came from. The clue is the missing credit: the words belong to someone else, but nobody is told. «Maya copied three paragraphs from a website and handed it in as her own essay.» Maya did not write those sentences and did not say where they came from, so her essay is plagiarised.
explain: The word can also describe the work itself. «The plagiarised project got no marks at all.» Here plagiarised is an adjective: it tells you what kind of project it was. Look for the clue: the work was taken from someone else and not owned up to.
explain: Schools take this seriously. Copying a classmate's homework, or pasting text from the internet, is cheating. To avoid it, put the idea into your own words and name your source, such as a book or a website. Then the work is honest, and the credit is yours.
fact: Plagiarised means copied from someone else's work and passed off as your own.
fact: Plagiarised is a verb past form: plagiarise, plagiarises, plagiarised, plagiarising. In American English it is spelled plagiarize.
fact: As an adjective, «plagiarised» describes work that was copied this way, as in «a plagiarised essay».
fact: Phrases you may meet are «plagiarised from the internet» and «plagiarised from a classmate».
fact: In school, plagiarism is cheating. To avoid it, use your own words and say where facts came from.
exercise: «Sam copied his friend's project word for word and handed it in as his own.» What has Sam done? | Translated it | Summarised it | *Plagiarised it | Revised it
exercise: «The teacher said Ellie's essay was plagiarised from a website, so it got no marks.» What does plagiarised describe? | Work written in pencil | Work that was finished early | *Work copied from someone else without saying so | Work with a lovely cover and neat pictures
exercise: How can you avoid plagiarism in your homework? | Copy the whole page and change one word | Hand in a classmate's homework with your name on it | *Put the ideas into your own words and name your source | Leave out the sources so nobody can check them
test: Which word means copied from someone else and passed off as your own? | Gentle | *Plagiarised | Original | Patient
test: «Ravi pasted three paragraphs from the internet and did not say where they came from.» Is this plagiarism? | No, because the internet is free to use | No, because he changed the title | *Yes, because he did not say where the words came from | Only if he copied from a printed book
exam: «The plagiarised report came back with a note: please write in your own words and name each source.» What does the note ask Ella to do? | Give the report to a friend to read | Hand the same report in again without changes | *Write in her own words and name her sources | Copy more pages from the internet
